=== STEP 0 (CREDENTIAL-LOGGER CHECK) TRIAGE MODE ===
I run step0_check (.sh on Linux, .ps1 on Windows) BEFORE typing any new password. It
changes nothing and prints [CLEAN] or [CHECK] lines + a RESULT. I'll paste the output;
walk me through it item by item. Adds to the network context I already gave you.

--- THE GOLDEN RULE ---
Never type or change a password on a box until every CAPTURE-RISK [CHECK] is fixed AND
step0 has been re-run and comes back clean for that item. If a logger is live, changing
the password just hands the attacker the new one — and the PCR I file makes it official.

--- SORT EVERY [CHECK] INTO ONE OF TWO BUCKETS ---
BUCKET A = STOP. It can intercept the password as I type/change it. Fix it NOW, re-run
           step0, THEN change the password. Do NOT defer these to the type1 script —
           type1 runs AFTER the password change, which is too late for a capture risk.
BUCKET B = PROCEED. It exposes creds already in memory but does not intercept the change
           itself. Safe to change the password now; note it for type1/type2 to clean up.

IMPORTANT: the type1 scripts auto-fix ONLY two of these — WDigest (Windows) and
/etc/ld.so.preload (Linux). Everything else step0 flags is a MANUAL fix. And even
ld.so.preload is a capture risk, so remove it now rather than waiting for type1.

--- LINUX step0 items ---
[CHECK] /etc/ld.so.preload exists      -> BUCKET A. Injected into every binary; can log
        passwd. Back it up, `rm /etc/ld.so.preload`, re-run step0. (type1 also removes it,
        but not in time — do it now.)
[CHECK] PAM pam_exec/expose_authtok / sufficient pam_permit
                                       -> BUCKET A, most careful. pam_exec/expose_authtok
        grabs the password during auth. Fix by hand in /etc/pam.d — KEEP A SECOND ROOT
        SHELL OPEN (a bad PAM edit locks you out). Remove the malicious line, test login
        in the second shell, re-run step0.
[CHECK] sudo log_input / iolog         -> BUCKET A. I change passwords via `sudo chpasswd`,
        so sudo I/O logging captures them. Remove the log_input/iolog directive from
        /etc/sudoers(.d) with `visudo`, re-run step0.
[CHECK] suspicious alias/function/PROMPT_COMMAND (sudo/passwd/chpasswd/ssh/su)
                                       -> BUCKET A, usually quick. A wrapper is shadowing
        the real command. Easiest safe fix: open a CLEAN shell and use absolute paths so
        no session hook applies — `env -i /bin/bash --noprofile --norc`, then
        `/usr/sbin/chpasswd`, `/usr/bin/sudo`. Then re-run step0.
[CHECK] sudo/chpasswd/passwd resolves to a non-standard path
                                       -> BUCKET A. A trojan earlier in $PATH is
        intercepting. Find/remove it, or use the absolute real-binary path as above, re-run.
[CHECK] sshd/sudo/pam binary differs from package ('5' lines)
                                       -> BUCKET A, most serious. The auth binary itself is
        trojaned. Reinstall ONLY that package (targeted, not a full upgrade), e.g.
        `apt install --reinstall openssh-server` / `dnf reinstall sudo`. Re-run step0;
        if it's still dirty, treat the box as deeply compromised.

--- WINDOWS step0 items ---
[CHECK] LSA Notification Packages has extras (password filter)
                                       -> BUCKET A, the big one. A password-filter DLL gets
        EVERY password change in cleartext. Remove the rogue name from the multi-string
        value, delete the DLL, and REBOOT (it stays loaded in LSASS until reboot). Only
        after reboot + a clean re-check do I change any password.
[CHECK] mimilsa.log exists (mimikatz SSP)   -> BUCKET A. A credential-logging SSP is live.
        Remove it from LSA Security Packages, delete mimilsa.log + the DLL, REBOOT, re-check.
[CHECK] LSA Security Packages has extras     -> BUCKET A. Same treatment as above (reboot).
[CHECK] PowerShell transcription is ON       -> BUCKET A, quick. I run `net user` in
        PowerShell, so transcription writes my new passwords to its output dir. Turn it off
        (remove the Transcription policy key / EnableTranscripting=0), note the OutputDir to
        clean later, re-run step0.
[CHECK] WDigest UseLogonCredential = 1        -> BUCKET B. Exposes cached creds in memory
        but doesn't intercept the change. Set it to 0 now (one reg line) if easy — and
        type1 sets it to 0 anyway. Not a reason to hold up the password change.

